# Overview
#
# A chatbot phrase frames text as a chat reply and says nothing about
# the subject (`Certainly! Here is the fix`, `hope this helps`). The
# reader gains nothing from it. The fix deletes the phrase and states
# the point. The rule sits at Required, because it matches exact phrases
# and the fix is mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists the phrases. `here's the updated` and the other `here is
# the` phrases return a reply to the asker. `apologies for`, `I
# apologize`, and `sorry for the confusion` answer a complaint, and
# `excellent question` and `glad you asked` praise the asker.
#
# Weir reads `!` as negation, so a pattern cannot name a literal
# exclamation mark. `certainly` and `absolutely` count instead when no
# space or other punctuation follows them. The `!` of a chat opener is
# the mark that remains (`Certainly! Here is the fix`). A space in a
# pattern is a whitespace token, so the guard sits glued to the word and
# reads the token right after it. A closing paren cannot sit in the list
# of the guard, because it ends the list.
#
# `you're right`, `that's a good point`, and `you're welcome` count only
# with punctuation glued after them (`You're right, the cache was
# stale`). `good catch` and `great catch` skip a following code word
# from `code`.
#
# False Positives
#
# The guard on `you're right` passes `you're right-clicking` and `you're
# right next to the limit`. The same guard on the other two phrases
# passes `a good point to set a breakpoint` and `you are welcome to open
# an issue`. `a good catch block` passes, because `catch` there names a
# part of the code.
#
# `(absolutely)` still draws a finding, because the guard cannot list a
# closing paren. `certainly` or `absolutely` at the end of a line draws
# one too.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProseChatbot holds the phrases that human-written
# READMEs also use, such as `feel free to`. This tier holds every other
# chatbot phrase. `I would be happy to help` draws a finding from each
# tier, because this tier reports `I would be happy to` and the
# Recommended tier reports `happy to help`.
#
# `of course` sits in the Required ProseFiller, so it draws one finding.
# ProseBroadReference leaves `helps` out of its verb list, so only this
# rule reports `hope this helps`. ProseModal skips `would` before `be
# happy`, so it leaves `I would be happy to` to this rule.
# ProseExclamation reports the `!` after a phrase of this rule, so
# `Certainly! Here is the fix` draws a finding from each rule, and no
# guard separates the two.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 1 line in the measure.py corpus, and 0 of 1 sampled hits
# was a chat reply. The misfire asked the reader to test a guess (`check
# to see if you're right!`). The phrases for apologies and praise hit
# none.
expr code [block, blocks, clause, clauses, statement, statements, handler, handlers, arm, arms]
expr main [(hope this helps), (hope that helps), (hope [this, that] [clarifies, clears]), (great question), (good question), ([great, good] catch!(( )@code)), ([you're, (you are)] right!([( ), -, /])), ([here's, (here is), (here are)] the [updated, revised, corrected, fixed]), (you're absolutely right), (you are absolutely right), (I'd be happy to), (I would be happy to), ([certainly, absolutely]![( ), ,, ., ?, ;, :, -, /]), (apologies for), (I apologize), (sorry for the confusion), ([(that's), (that is)] a [great, good, fair, valid, excellent] point![( ), -, /]), ([excellent, fantastic] question), ([(you're), (you are)] welcome![( ), -, /]), (glad you asked)]
let message "Chatbot phrase. Delete it and respond directly."
let description "Flags phrases that frame a reply as a chat instead of stating the point."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The absolutely-positioned element overlaps the header."
allows "The fix is certainly correct."
allows "Is it safe? Certainly."
allows "A good catch block logs the error before it rethrows."
allows "Make sure you're right-clicking the tray icon."
allows "That is a good point to set a breakpoint."
allows "You are welcome to open an issue."
